  <title>Print a label</title>

  <p>LinuxZPL sends labels straight to a Zebra printer on the network, with
  no printing system in between. The printer has to be reachable from this
  computer at its address and port; if it is not, the error says why.</p>

  <section id="default-printer">
    <title>The printer you normally use</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>Settings</gui><gui>Default Printer…</gui></guiseq> holds
    the printer's <gui>Address</gui>, <gui>Port</gui> (usually 9100) and
    <gui>DPI</gui>, its resolution. <gui>Test Connection</gui> checks the
    printer can be reached and asks it its resolution. <gui>Font
    memory</gui> says where on the printer fonts are kept: see
    <link xref="fonts"/>. These are remembered from one session to the
    next.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="print-window">
    <title>The Print window</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>File</gui><gui>Print…</gui></guiseq>
    (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>P</key></keyseq>) opens the Print window.
    <gui>Print</gui> prints, <gui>OK</gui> keeps the settings without
    printing, and <gui>Cancel</gui> changes nothing.</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Printer</gui></title>
        <p>The printer to print to. A change here lasts until LinuxZPL is
        closed and is not remembered; <gui>Use Default</gui> fills in the
        default printer again. This is how to print to another printer for
        a while.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Copies (^PQ)</gui></title>
        <p>How many labels to print, and optionally a pause after every so
        many (<gui>Pause every</gui>, 0 for never). The printer prints the
        copies itself. <gui>Replicates</gui> prints each serial number that
        many times before moving to the next.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Label</gui></title>
        <p><gui>Print upside down</gui>, <gui>Mirror left to right</gui>,
        <gui>Reverse fields, white on black</gui>, and <gui>Check barcode
        data as printed</gui>, which has the printer check each barcode's data
        and print an error message in place of one that is not valid.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Printer setup sent with the label</gui></title>
        <p><gui>Darkness</gui>, the print, slew and backfeed speeds, the
        print mode (such as tear off, peel off or cutter), the media
        tracking and the media type. Each starts at <gui>Printer's
        setting</gui>, which sends nothing and leaves the printer as it was
        set up.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>Everything but the printer is saved with the label, so the label
    prints the same way next time, and a change to it can be undone.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="quick-print">
    <title>Quick Print</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>File</gui><gui>Quick Print</gui></guiseq>
    (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Shift</key><key>P</key></keyseq>) prints at
    once, to the printer in use and with the label's settings as they
    are.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="before">
    <title>Before it prints</title>
    <list>
      <item><p>If the label uses fonts of its own, LinuxZPL asks the printer
      whether it has them, and offers to send any it lacks: see
      <link xref="fonts"/>.</p></item>
      <item><p>Elements whose <gui>Print This Element</gui> is cleared are
      not printed.</p></item>
      <item><p>The label always tells the printer whether to print upside
      down, mirrored or reversed, and which character set it is in, since a
      printer otherwise keeps whatever the last job set.</p></item>
    </list>
  </section>
</page>
